WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.280
-: So, now that we've seen how to store data

00:02.280 --> 00:04.950
in a very low-level way in Git.

00:04.950 --> 00:06.990
Normally, we use a whole bunch of other commands,

00:06.990 --> 00:08.670
and Git hashes things for us,

00:08.670 --> 00:10.207
but we can manually tell it,

00:10.207 --> 00:13.200
"Hash this, turn it into a Git object, and store it."

00:13.200 --> 00:15.630
So, we've done that a couple of times,

00:15.630 --> 00:17.700
and we got these keys back.

00:17.700 --> 00:20.013
Right, we have this one and this one.

00:20.910 --> 00:23.760
So, imagine that I don't know what was stored in there,

00:23.760 --> 00:26.370
or it was a really long file that I stored.

00:26.370 --> 00:28.620
I want to retrieve that data out.

00:28.620 --> 00:30.030
I have the key,

00:30.030 --> 00:32.430
and Git is storing the information in these files.

00:32.430 --> 00:34.260
I just can't read them.

00:34.260 --> 00:36.750
So, to get it out, there's a function,

00:36.750 --> 00:40.297
or there's a Git command called "cat-file,"

00:40.297 --> 00:44.130
"git cat-file," and then we pass in an object-hash.

00:44.130 --> 00:45.720
So that could be a commit hash,

00:45.720 --> 00:47.070
or it could be one of these hashes.

00:47.070 --> 00:48.510
This is not from a commit;

00:48.510 --> 00:49.890
it's from something called a blob.

00:49.890 --> 00:52.050
We'll go into more detail.

00:52.050 --> 00:54.570
But any of these hashes we get in Git

00:54.570 --> 00:58.560
they all are tied to a Git Object, one of four types.

00:58.560 --> 01:00.330
And we can get information,

01:00.330 --> 01:03.720
we can read them out using git cat-file.

01:03.720 --> 01:06.210
Now this -p option tells Git

01:06.210 --> 01:08.160
to pretty-print the contents of the object

01:08.160 --> 01:10.680
based on the type of object it is.

01:10.680 --> 01:13.470
So, I could pretty-print a commit

01:13.470 --> 01:15.750
if I had a commit, using -p,

01:15.750 --> 01:18.570
it would detect it to commit and print it out as a commit.

01:18.570 --> 01:20.970
But we're gonna try it with what we have.

01:20.970 --> 01:22.320
These are not commits.

01:22.320 --> 01:26.743
So I can copy the whole hash and do a git cat-file -p,

01:29.400 --> 01:32.040
and then I'll pass in the whole thing just to start.

01:32.040 --> 01:33.330
And what do you know?

01:33.330 --> 01:35.520
It says, "Hey, I have something stored for that.

01:35.520 --> 01:36.930
I have, hello."

01:36.930 --> 01:39.330
I can also give it way fewer, right?

01:39.330 --> 01:40.740
I could just give it a couple of digits,

01:40.740 --> 01:41.970
and it can figure it out.

01:41.970 --> 01:44.520
Let's do our second value that we stored,

01:44.520 --> 01:45.540
which was "goodbye."

01:45.540 --> 01:47.580
We got back "dd7e1."

01:47.580 --> 01:48.840
Let's just do that.

01:48.840 --> 01:50.820
So instead of the whole hash,

01:50.820 --> 01:54.060
just that little bit, and it knows what it stored,

01:54.060 --> 01:57.810
we gave it the key, it gave us back "goodbye."

01:57.810 --> 01:58.710
All right.

01:58.710 --> 02:01.530
So, now what I wanna show you is a little bit different.

02:01.530 --> 02:02.910
I wanna use files.

02:02.910 --> 02:06.570
I wanna have, actually, just two versions of the same file,

02:06.570 --> 02:09.600
both of which we'll be able to store manually

02:09.600 --> 02:12.570
using the command git hash-object.

02:12.570 --> 02:15.180
And then we'll be able to get those versions back out.

02:15.180 --> 02:16.800
So let's make our new file.

02:16.800 --> 02:20.550
I'll just touch dogs.txt.

02:20.550 --> 02:22.980
And then I'm gonna open it up in my editor.

02:22.980 --> 02:24.360
Alright, here it is.

02:24.360 --> 02:28.020
And I'll put in some of my dogs,

02:28.020 --> 02:30.480
Rusty and Wyatt,

02:30.480 --> 02:32.550
rest in peace, Rusty and Wyatt.

02:32.550 --> 02:35.580
All right, so I have one version of this file.

02:35.580 --> 02:40.410
If I look at that file, that's what we have in that file.

02:40.410 --> 02:43.500
So, I can tell Git to hash it,

02:43.500 --> 02:44.670
I can do the same thing,

02:44.670 --> 02:45.720
git hash-object.

02:45.720 --> 02:48.960
I don't have to bother with the echo and the standard in

02:48.960 --> 02:51.120
because I'm gonna provide it a file.

02:51.120 --> 02:54.480
I want it to tell me what is the hypothetical hash

02:54.480 --> 02:56.280
I would get back if you stored this?

02:56.280 --> 02:59.190
I'm not storing it 'cause I didn't do -w.

02:59.190 --> 03:00.480
That's what we get back.

03:00.480 --> 03:03.210
So, assuming that file doesn't change...

03:03.210 --> 03:04.140
Remember how this works.

03:04.140 --> 03:06.270
The same input gives you the same output.

03:06.270 --> 03:09.330
Well, that's really important for how Git works

03:09.330 --> 03:10.867
because it can very quickly tell,

03:10.867 --> 03:12.180
"Oh, this file hasn't changed.

03:12.180 --> 03:14.910
It's giving me the same hash that it had before."

03:14.910 --> 03:16.980
But as soon as I change anything,

03:16.980 --> 03:18.810
anything, a single space,

03:18.810 --> 03:20.760
a capital letter to a lowercase,

03:20.760 --> 03:23.580
the entire hash changes, and Git can detect that.

03:23.580 --> 03:26.340
Anyway, I'm going to store it now.

03:26.340 --> 03:28.830
So, I'm gonna add the -w.

03:28.830 --> 03:29.663
All right.

03:29.663 --> 03:34.080
So, git hash-dogs.txt -w.

03:34.080 --> 03:36.600
And if you're following along here,

03:36.600 --> 03:39.180
you can type this exactly as you see it

03:39.180 --> 03:41.070
or just make note of, you know,

03:41.070 --> 03:43.500
the fact that your hash is different

03:43.500 --> 03:47.130
and just use that hash when we come back to it.

03:47.130 --> 03:49.170
So, just be consistent, right?

03:49.170 --> 03:50.760
If you do follow along with me,

03:50.760 --> 03:52.230
make sure you're getting this hash.

03:52.230 --> 03:53.970
If you don't, it's not a big deal.

03:53.970 --> 03:55.650
Just use that hash that you're getting

03:55.650 --> 03:57.270
instead of the one that I have.

03:57.270 --> 03:58.103
All right?

03:58.103 --> 03:59.460
So, I'm gonna do -w,

03:59.460 --> 04:03.840
I'm saying store this version of that file, dogs.txt.

04:03.840 --> 04:05.790
And I'll have to refresh VS Code,

04:05.790 --> 04:09.069
but there it is, "39 e27..." whatever.

04:09.069 --> 04:11.227
And that's what it starts with, right?

04:11.227 --> 04:14.040
"39e27..." blah blah blah.

04:14.040 --> 04:16.110
So now, if I do a different version...

04:16.110 --> 04:19.290
If I change what's in this file, Rusty, Wyatt,

04:19.290 --> 04:21.290
pretend, you know, I'm using Git.

04:21.290 --> 04:23.940
We we haven't made any commits to be clear, right?

04:23.940 --> 04:27.870
If I type git log, no commits.

04:27.870 --> 04:31.650
But if I add another dog on here, Rusty, Wyatt,

04:31.650 --> 04:34.860
I had a childhood dog named Cheyenne,

04:34.860 --> 04:39.390
and how about Sirius Black, another great dog.

04:39.390 --> 04:40.223
Okay?

04:40.223 --> 04:42.390
So, a new version of this file,

04:42.390 --> 04:44.340
if I try hashing that,

04:44.340 --> 04:46.560
I won't store it just yet, but if I hash it,

04:46.560 --> 04:48.780
we get a very different hash now

04:48.780 --> 04:50.490
because things have changed.

04:50.490 --> 04:52.710
Small change, any change should lead to

04:52.710 --> 04:54.630
a large change in the output.

04:54.630 --> 04:55.980
So, still 40 characters,

04:55.980 --> 04:59.610
that hash that we get back from trial one from Git,

04:59.610 --> 05:01.440
but it's very different.

05:01.440 --> 05:03.300
So, let's store that one too.

05:03.300 --> 05:04.133
All right?

05:04.133 --> 05:08.884
So, I'm gonna do git hash-object dogs.txt -w for right.

05:08.884 --> 05:12.150
So, we should see a new folder called FD,

05:12.150 --> 05:15.363
and a file called this "915," and so on.

05:16.920 --> 05:19.620
So, whatever you did, if you had changes,

05:19.620 --> 05:21.630
you should get a different hash.

05:21.630 --> 05:24.420
And in that .GIT/objects folder,

05:24.420 --> 05:25.950
there now is an FD,

05:25.950 --> 05:29.040
at least in my example, 915.

05:29.040 --> 05:32.970
So, I can't read it, but we can use the cat command, right?

05:32.970 --> 05:36.090
Git cat-file, which if I scroll up...

05:36.090 --> 05:37.080
Where did that go?

05:37.080 --> 05:39.390
We used it recently, maybe not.

05:39.390 --> 05:41.368
Anyways, there it is, cat-file.

05:41.368 --> 05:44.220
And then I can provide that hash.

05:44.220 --> 05:46.080
So, either one of these hashes.

05:46.080 --> 05:48.753
If I do this, let's take this one.

05:49.590 --> 05:52.500
Git cat-file, and I provide that hash...

05:52.500 --> 05:53.400
Well, it yells at me

05:53.400 --> 05:56.880
'cause I forgot to provide the -p option for pretty-print.

05:56.880 --> 05:57.900
There we are.

05:57.900 --> 05:59.910
So, we see the first version.

05:59.910 --> 06:02.280
Actually, this is the second version, rather.

06:02.280 --> 06:03.960
But if I take the other one,

06:03.960 --> 06:05.310
and this is where it gets kind of annoying

06:05.310 --> 06:06.143
to watch me do this,

06:06.143 --> 06:08.430
but remember I have two versions.

06:08.430 --> 06:10.860
This was the second one, this was the first one.

06:10.860 --> 06:12.633
I'll just copy part of that hash.

06:13.800 --> 06:15.240
And then I'll do the same thing,

06:15.240 --> 06:19.263
git cat-file -p, I forgot that.

06:20.310 --> 06:21.870
This was the other version.

06:21.870 --> 06:24.300
So, Git is storing both of these.

06:24.300 --> 06:26.670
I can delete the file entirely.

06:26.670 --> 06:29.940
I could get rid of this file, dogs.txt,

06:29.940 --> 06:32.040
but as long as I don't delete .git,

06:32.040 --> 06:34.740
it still has both of these versions in there,

06:34.740 --> 06:36.990
and I can get them out without even making a commit.

06:36.990 --> 06:39.960
I told get to store those things for me manually.

06:39.960 --> 06:42.810
So, let's say I delete everything from...

06:42.810 --> 06:44.100
Let's just delete the contents.

06:44.100 --> 06:46.140
I won't delete the entire file.

06:46.140 --> 06:47.460
Okay?

06:47.460 --> 06:50.100
If I wanted to restore the contents,

06:50.100 --> 06:52.140
I'll take the hash that we got back,

06:52.140 --> 06:56.040
this one that starts with "fd9," just like that.

06:56.040 --> 06:58.050
Whatever you got back, copy it.

06:58.050 --> 06:59.580
And then lemme clear this.

06:59.580 --> 07:03.480
I'm gonna do a git and then cat-file -p,

07:03.480 --> 07:04.830
pass in that hash.

07:04.830 --> 07:08.340
But this time I'm going to add it into that file.

07:08.340 --> 07:10.380
Is it dogs, dog?

07:10.380 --> 07:12.160
Dogs.txt

07:14.490 --> 07:15.323
And take a look.

07:15.323 --> 07:17.190
It's been restored for me.

07:17.190 --> 07:19.740
And if I use the other hash, which I can't remember,

07:19.740 --> 07:21.870
but I could even just look in here.

07:21.870 --> 07:26.340
I think it was "39e275."

07:26.340 --> 07:27.720
We'll see if I'm right or not.

07:27.720 --> 07:28.553
Let's see.

07:28.553 --> 07:29.697
"39e275."

07:31.565 --> 07:34.650
There it is, Rusty and Wyatt.

07:34.650 --> 07:36.847
So, what I'm doing here is telling Git,

07:36.847 --> 07:38.940
"Please go and take this key,

07:38.940 --> 07:42.330
find the corresponding value that was stored under that key

07:42.330 --> 07:44.760
and then take the contents of that file."

07:44.760 --> 07:45.593
Right?

07:45.593 --> 07:46.426
Each one of these files

07:46.426 --> 07:48.450
it's binary, it's compressed, it's encrypted.

07:48.450 --> 07:51.480
Take that, turn it back into something that makes sense.

07:51.480 --> 07:54.120
And then stick it in dogs.txt.

07:54.120 --> 07:55.590
So, I could still delete that file,

07:55.590 --> 07:56.423
I could empty it out.

07:56.423 --> 07:58.170
I could have some radical changes in here.

07:58.170 --> 08:00.467
Which I mean we do this all the time with Git, right?

08:00.467 --> 08:02.670
And then we realize, "Oh shoot,

08:02.670 --> 08:05.070
I wanna go back to an older version of this file."

08:05.070 --> 08:06.754
Well, Git stores them all in here,

08:06.754 --> 08:09.210
not because we're running this command

08:09.210 --> 08:11.430
or because we're running git hash-object,

08:11.430 --> 08:13.230
but it's the same principle.

08:13.230 --> 08:15.330
Git is able to hold onto those files.

08:15.330 --> 08:17.760
It's storing them in that objects directory.

08:17.760 --> 08:19.837
And then at any point we can say,

08:19.837 --> 08:22.830
"Okay, please go give us that."

08:22.830 --> 08:25.833
Let me redirect that into dogs.txt,

08:26.760 --> 08:28.270
and we're back to normal.

08:28.270 --> 08:32.010
Okay, so we're one step closer to understanding things.

08:32.010 --> 08:34.020
We have not made a single commit,

08:34.020 --> 08:35.490
just wanna be very clear.

08:35.490 --> 08:38.340
We have only hashed things, hashed files,

08:38.340 --> 08:41.550
and hash strings of text and stored those,

08:41.550 --> 08:43.350
and we've been able to retrieve them

08:43.350 --> 08:45.720
using the hashes we get back.

08:45.720 --> 08:47.010
But that's not a commit.

08:47.010 --> 08:48.750
In fact, there's something called a blob.

08:48.750 --> 08:51.813
And in the next video we'll expand more on blobs.
